**Answer: Yes, $f \equiv 0$.**

**Proof.** Suppose for contradiction that $f$ is not identically zero. Then there exists a point $x_0 \in \Omega$ with $f(x_0) \neq 0$. Replacing $f$ by $-f$ if necessary, assume $f(x_0) > 0$.

Since $f$ is continuous, there is a radius $r > 0$ with $B(x_0, r) \Subset \Omega$ such that
$$f(x) > \tfrac{1}{2}f(x_0) > 0 \quad\text{for all } x \in B(x_0, r).$$

Now construct a test function $g$. Let
$$\psi(t) = \begin{cases} e^{-1/(1-t^2)}, & |t| < 1, \\ 0, & |t| \ge 1. \end{cases}$$
This is the standard smooth bump: $\psi \in C^\infty(\mathbb{R})$, $\psi \ge 0$, and $\psi(0) = e^{-1} > 0$. Define on $\mathbb{R}^n$
$$g(x) = \psi\!\left(\frac{\lVert x - x_0\rVert}{r/2}\right).$$
Then $g \in C^\infty_c(\mathbb{R}^n) \subseteq C^k_c(\Omega)$ (smooth functions are $C^k$ for every $k$), $\operatorname{supp} g \subseteq \overline{B(x_0, r/2)} \subset B(x_0,r)$, $g \ge 0$ everywhere, and $g > 0$ on $B(x_0, r/2)$.

By hypothesis,
$$\int_\Omega f(x)\,g(x)\,dx = 0.$$
But the integrand $fg$ is continuous on $\Omega$, non-negative on $\operatorname{supp} g \subset B(x_0,r)$, and strictly positive on the open set $B(x_0, r/2)$ (where both $f > \tfrac12 f(x_0) > 0$ and $g > 0$). A continuous non-negative function that is strictly positive on a non-empty open set has strictly positive integral. Hence
$$\int_\Omega f(x)\,g(x)\,dx > 0,$$
contradicting the hypothesis.

Therefore no such $x_0$ exists, and $f \equiv 0$ on $\Omega$. $\blacksquare$

